    "body": "> See also [openui/open-ui#1148](https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/1148) which touches on the first part.\n> \n> I'm on the one hand quite supportive of that kind of implicit commandfor. It's similar in some ways to how a label wrapping an input labels it. Or how the first legend in a field set associates with it. Also submit and reset buttons already behave like this with their implicit form owner relationships.\n> \n> I do wonder if potentially the actual cases are limited though, essentially it's close or request-close within a , and hide-popover within a [popover]. And feasibly other similar commands that close things in future.\n> \n> Would this work across shadow roots?\n\nYou mean something like this? Not sure. Does this work for implicit label or submit refs?\n```html\n<dialog>\n  <template shadowrootmode=open>\n     <button command=close>Close</button>\n  </template>\n</dialog>\n```\n> I would be curious if there's something that can be done for custom elements such that they could implicitly be a target for custom commands?\n\nThat would be really interesting. \n\n> \n> I'm less convinced by the commandscope bit but that's just initial feeling.\n\nI think that for some use cases having some sort of implicit local scope that doesn't rely on ID would be great, but I am also not sure yet if a boolean attribute is the way to go.",
